<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borgund Stave Church</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Borgund Stave Church is a wooden church in Lærdal in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, built around 1180–1250 and dedicated to the apostle Andrew. With its tiered roofs, dragon-head gables and carved portals it is the best-preserved of Norway's medieval stave churches and the model for many later copies. It ceased to be a parish church in 1868 and is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borgund-stave-church.jpg" alt="Borgund Stave Church" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ximonic (Simo Räsänen) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borgund_Stave_Church_in_L%C3%A6rdalen,_2013_June.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
